WEBVTT

cue-VID_U2_03-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Recruitment is the process from identifying that the business needs to employ someone up to the point at which applications have arrived at the business. Juniper Lane Coffee Roasters needs one regional manager. Forty-eight applications land in the inbox. The funnel narrows step by step until one person is hired. Follow each stage and check the evidence.

cue-VID_U2_03-2
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:50.000
The funnel starts with job analysis, which identifies and records the responsibilities and tasks of the job. Next comes a job description outlining duties, and a person specification listing qualifications, experience and personal qualities. The advert goes online. Application forms come in, then shortlisting, interviews, references and an offer. Each stage removes unsuitable candidates. Skip job analysis and a vague advert pulls the wrong people.

cue-VID_U2_03-3
00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Internal recruitment fills a vacancy with an existing employee; external recruitment brings someone new. Priya manages a cafe and knows the schedule. Priya can start in twelve days and costs six hundred dollars to train. Mark may bring fresh ideas, but takes thirty-five days and two thousand four hundred dollars. Internal is quicker and cheaper; external widens the talent pool.

cue-VID_U2_03-4
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Selection evaluates candidates against the job. Shortlisting cuts forty-eight CVs to twelve. References and a short aptitude test reduce twelve to four interviews. A panel scores answers against the person specification, not friendliness. One candidate receives the offer. An assessment centre could test planning under pressure, but costs more. The exam tip is simple: match your recommendation to evidence in the case, then justify it.

cue-VID_U2_03-5
00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:04.000
An employment contract is a legal agreement between employer and employee listing rights and responsibilities. It states duties, working hours, holiday entitlement and sick pay. Legal controls cover unfair dismissal, discrimination and safety, and set an eleven dollar fifty minimum wage. Equal opportunities rules require fair treatment. These rules raise employer costs but improve fairness and retention.

cue-VID_U2_03-6
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Three training lanes appear. Induction training introduces the business, its customs and colleagues. On-the-job training happens by watching and practising with an experienced worker at the workplace. Off-the-job training takes staff away to specialist trainers. Juniper budgets two hundred dollars for induction, six hundred for shadowing, and two thousand four hundred for a college course. On-the-job is cheap and immediate; off-the-job is broader but expensive.

cue-VID_U2_03-7
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:58.000
Juniper offers the role to Priya at fourteen dollars an hour. Because she already knows the twelve cafes, therefore induction is short, so training costs six hundred dollars instead of two thousand four hundred. The vacancy is filled in twelve days, not thirty-five. Week one covers food safety and the till. Mark would have brought new ideas, but the saving is eighteen hundred dollars and the role opens twenty-three days sooner.

cue-VID_U2_03-8
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Now separate dismissal from redundancy. Redundancy happens when a job is no longer needed; dismissal usually follows poor conduct or capability. Unfair dismissal, such as sacking someone for joining a union, is illegal. Two cafes close, so redundant staff receive compensation and retraining. Weigh the internal hire: fast, cheap and motivating, but it limits new ideas. The external hire widens talent, yet costs more and takes longer. Good answers state one benefit, one limitation and a justified choice.

cue-VID_U2_03-9
00:03:28.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Review the whole chain. Job analysis leads to a job description and person specification, then advertising, forms, shortlisting, interviews and an offer. Selection uses CVs, references, tests and assessment centres. Contracts and legal controls protect both sides. Induction, on-the-job and off-the-job training develop skills. Juniper chose Priya, saved eighteen hundred dollars and filled the post twenty-three days early. In your answer, name the stage, apply the case detail, and evaluate the trade-off. That is how a recommendation earns marks.

